ROCR-Runtime, GFXIP 7/8: restore the double-mapped AQL ring buffer
("queue_full_workaround_") that upstream removed along with the rest of
gfx7/8 support. Without it hsa_queue_create fails on gfx803 for every queue
larger than 64 packets, and a producer that fills a ring exactly parks the
CP for good.

WHY
---
On GFXIP 7/8 the CP cannot accept a doorbell that advances a full queue
length. The hardware write index is masked to the ring size, so
wptr == rptr + size is bit-for-bit indistinguishable from wptr == rptr --
an empty ring. When a producer fills the ring exactly, the packet processor
concludes there is nothing to do, parks, and never drains it again. Nothing
recovers it: the producer is already blocked waiting on a completion signal
for a packet the CP will never fetch, so it never rings another doorbell.

ROCm 6.4.4's ROCR states the mechanism and the fix in its own words
(runtime/hsa-runtime/core/runtime/amd_aql_queue.cpp, AqlQueue's ctor):

    // When queue_full_workaround_ is set to 1, the ring buffer is internally
    // doubled in size. Virtual addresses in the upper half of the ring
    // allocation are mapped to the same set of pages backing the lower half.
    // Values written to the HW doorbell are modulo the doubled size.
    // This allows the HW to accept (doorbell == last_doorbell + queue_size).
    // This workaround is required for GFXIP 7 and GFXIP 8 ASICs.

    queue_full_workaround_ =
        (isa->GetMajorVersion() == 7 || isa->GetMajorVersion() == 8) ? 1 : 0;

Doubling the *virtual* allocation over the same backing store gives the
masked index one more bit than the ring holds, which is what makes the full
case distinguishable from the empty one.

The kernel implements its entire half of this and expects userspace to ask
for it. amdgpu_amdkfd_gpuvm.c:

    /* Workaround for AQL queue wraparound bug. Map the same
     * memory twice. That means we only actually allocate half the memory. */
    if ((*mem)->aql_queue)
        size >>= 1;

and kfd_mem_attach() maps the one BO at both `va` and `va + bo_size`
(`for (i = 0; i <= is_aql; i++) { ...; va += bo_size; }`). The trigger is
HsaMemFlags.ui32.AQLQueueMemory -> KFD_IOC_ALLOC_MEM_FLAGS_AQL_QUEUE_MEM.
kfd_queue.c's queue-buffer validation carries the matching assumption:

    /* AQL queues on GFX7 and GFX8 appear twice their actual size */
    expected_queue_size = PAGE_ALIGN(properties->queue_size / 2);

ROCR does not ask. MemoryRegion::AllocateDoubleMap is commented
"Deprecated:", and only core/driver/virtio/amd_kfd_virtio_driver.cpp reads
it. KfdDriver::AllocateMemory does not translate it, so
HsaMemFlags.ui32.AQLQueueMemory is never set on a KFD allocation, and
AqlQueue::AllocRegisteredRingBuffer allocates a plain single-mapped ring of
exactly its packet capacity.

That one missing flag explains the 64-packet floor. For a ring of N bytes,
kfd_queue_buffer_get() expects a mapping of PAGE_ALIGN(N/2), which equals a
single-mapped N only when N is one page, 64 packets. Every larger queue
fails that exact-match test, and hsa_queue_create returns an error. The
two halves of the flag do not work apart: reporting 2N without the mirror
lets hsa_queue_create succeed at full size and then faults the GPU under
load ("Memory access fault ... Page not present", "HW Exception ... reason:
GPU Hang"), because the CP addresses a 2N span whose upper half is not
mapped, and forcing the HQD back to N (REFERENCE-amdkfd-gfx7-8-queue-size-
writeback) removes the fault together with the workaround.

The full-ring park is not a store-ordering bug. On gfx803 the ring's CPU
mapping is write-back: amdgpu sets no AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_CPU_GTT_USWC on the
KFD path (amdgpu_amdkfd_gpuvm.c: `alloc_flags = 0` for GTT), and gmc_v8 does
not read AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_UNCACHED. x86 store order already puts the
packet stores ahead of the doorbell store, so the store fences before the
doorbell writes in StoreRelaxed and SdmaQueue::RingDoorbell add no ordering
on this card.

WHAT
----
- memory_region.h: drop the "Deprecated:" comment on AllocateDoubleMap.
- amd_kfd_driver.cpp: KfdDriver::AllocateMemory translates
  AllocateDoubleMap to HsaMemFlags.ui32.AQLQueueMemory, as ROCm 6.4.4's
  KfdDriver::AllocateMemory did.
- amd_aql_queue.h: add queue_full_workaround_.
- amd_aql_queue.cpp: set it for GFXIP 7/8; request AllocateDoubleMap for the
  system-memory ring next to the AllocateNonPaged it already requests;
  publish the doubled VA span in ring_buf_alloc_bytes_ so CreateQueue and
  UpdateQueue describe the real span to the CP; raise the minimum ring to
  one page and halve the maximum (the alias is not usable capacity); mask
  the legacy type-0 doorbell against the doubled size; and reject the
  device-memory ring path on GFXIP 7/8, which cannot be double-mapped.

The ring is NonPaged, because kfd_queue_buffer_get() resolves it through
amdgpu_vm_bo_lookup_mapping() and needs a BO. libhsakmt's
fmm_allocate_host_gpu() therefore allocates it as a GTT BO through
__fmm_allocate_device(). With AQLQueueMemory set it reserves twice the VA,
fmm_translate_hsa_to_ioc_flags() adds KFD_IOC_ALLOC_MEM_FLAGS_AQL_QUEUE_MEM,
and the kernel halves the BO and maps it at `va` and `va + bo_size`. The CPU
mapping covers the first half, which is the whole real ring.

CLR requests a device-memory ring only on gfx94x and gfx125x
(Settings::setKernelArgImpl in rocsettings.cpp), so the GFXIP 7/8 rejection
of that path does not trigger for HIP on gfx803.

Build it against a kernel WITHOUT REFERENCE-amdkfd-gfx7-8-queue-size-writeback,
which forces the HQD back to the single ring size and cancels the double
map, and never combine it with graph-replay-queue-size-cap.patch. A tree
that doubles the reported size twice makes KFD reject every queue with
`expected size 0x2000 not equal to mapping addr ... size 0x1000` in dmesg.

Depends on: hsa-agent-rejects-legacy-doorbell.patch (applies on top of it;
queue_full_workaround_ multiplies the legacy type-0 doorbell mask that patch
restores).

Supersedes: graph-replay-queue-size-cap.patch and its out-of-tree kernel
companion REFERENCE-amdkfd-gfx7-8-queue-size-writeback.patch. Neither may be
applied alongside this one -- see their headers.

Applies to: rocm-systems release/therock-10.2, commit
43fa692e568e65a1fb84f2d1c74415b042839f10.

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (gfx803 box, 2026-08-28) on ROCm 7.14, where the
ring was a userptr BO: the maximum queue size goes from 64 to 131072
packets, and rocclr's live compute queue from 64 to 16384. This patch does
not fix the silent dispatch hang: that hang reproduces with this patch and a
16384-packet ring, and it comes from VRAM clocked above its rating (see
"Required host setup" in README.md).

NOT YET RE-VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE on ROCm 10.2, where the ring is a GTT
BO. To verify, on a kernel without REFERENCE-amdkfd-gfx7-8-queue-size-writeback
and in an image built from the commit above: hsa_queue_create must succeed
for every power of two from 64 packets up to the agent's
HSA_AGENT_INFO_QUEUE_MAX_SIZE (131072 on this card); dmesg must show no
`expected size ... not equal to mapping` line and no VM fault; and a
producer that fills a 64-packet queue exactly must keep draining.

